est failed. See above for more details.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784972557295},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Test fails as predicted. Now I need to implement the actual Game of Life logic. Let me think about the minimal implementation.\n\nFor Test 5 (block), I need the implementation to:\n1. Take 4 cells forming a 2x2 block\n2. Return those same 4 cells unchanged\n\nThe minimal implementation needs to:\n- Iterate through live cells\n- Count their neighbors\n- Apply survival rule (2 or 3 neighbors)\n\nFor a block, each cell has exactly 2 live neighbors. So all survive. No births because no dead cell has exactly 3 live neighbors.\n\nLet me implement the full Conway's Game of Life logic now (since I'll need it for subsequent tests anyway, but I should still keep it minimal):\n\n```typescript\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  const liveSet = new Set(cells.map(([x, y]) => `${x},${y}`));\n  const neighborCounts = new Map<string, [number, number]>();\n  \n  for (const [x, y] of cells) {\n    for (let dx = -1; dx <= 1; dx++) {\n      for (let dy = -1; dy <= 1; dy++) {\n        if (dx === 0 && dy === 0) continue;\n        const nx = x + dx;\n        const ny = y + dy;\n        const key = `${nx},${ny}`;\n        neighborCounts.set(key, (neighborCounts.get(key) ?? 0) + 1);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  \n  const next: Cell[] = [];\n  const checked = new Set<string>();\n  \n  for (const [x, y] of cells) {\n    const key = `${x},${y}`;\n    checked.add(key);\n    const count = neighborCounts.get(key) ?? 0;\n    if (count === 2 || count === 3) {\n      next.push([x, y]);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  for (const [key, count] of neighborCounts) {\n    if (count === 3 && !checked.has(key)) {\n      const [nx, ny] = key.split(\",\").map(Number);\n      next.push([nx, ny]);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  return next;\n}\n```\n\nThis is a complete implementation. It will pass all subsequent tests too. But the green phase says \"hardcoded returns a