 count === 3);\n\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  const live = new Set(cells.map(toKey));\n  const counts = new Map<string, number>();\n  \n  for (const [x, y] of cells) {\n    for (const [dx, dy] of NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS) {\n      const key = `${x + dx},${y + dy}`;\n      counts.set(key, (counts.get(key) ?? 0) + 1);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  const next: Cell[] = [];\n  for (const [key, count] of counts) {\n    if (survives(count, live.has(key))) next.push(fromKey(key));\n  }\n  return next;\n}\n```\n\nHmm, this might add more elements. Let me stick with the simpler refactor.\n\nActually, let me try yet another approach. What if I extract `countNeighbors` as a helper?\n\n```typescript\nfunction countNeighbors(cells: Cell[]): Map<string, number> {\n  const counts = new Map<string, number>();\n  for (const [x, y] of cells) {\n    for (const [dx, dy] of NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS) {\n      const key = `${x + dx},${y + dy}`;\n      counts.set(key, (counts.get(key) ?? 0) + 1);\n    }\n  }\n  return counts;\n}\n\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  const live = new Set(cells.map(toKey));\n  const counts = countNeighbors(cells);\n  const next: Cell[] = [];\n  for (const [key, count] of counts) {\n    const wasAlive = live.has(key);\n    if (count === 3 || (count === 2 && wasAlive)) {\n      next.push(fromKey(key));\n    }\n  }\n  return next;\n}\n```\n\nThis is cleaner. The main function is now very clear.\n\nOK let me decide. I'll go with the extraction approach because:\n- `countNeighbors` is a clear, named helper\n- The main function becomes very readable: count, then iterate\n- Reveals intent: the main function shows the Game of Life rules clearly\n\nLet me apply this.\n\nHmm, actually, looking at it more, the extraction adds elements (a new function). Is it worth it?\n\nPros of extraction:\n- `countNeighbors` is a reusable abstraction\n- Main function is shorter and clearer\n\nCons:\n- One more function to maintain\n- Inline would be just as clear\n\nLooking at the rules:\n- Rule 2 (intent): Both versions are reasonably clear\n- Rule 3 (DRY): No duplication either way\n- Rule 4 (fewest elements): Inline has fewer elements\n\nI think I'll do both: extract the helper AND unify the rules. This gives the clearest code.\n\nLet me write it out and run the tests.\n\nActually, let me reconsider once more. The cleanest refactor for this code is:\n\n```typescript\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  const live = new Set(cells.map(toKey));\n  const counts = new Map<string, number>();\n  \n  for (const [x, y] of cells) {\n    for (const [dx, dy] of NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS) {\n      const key = `${x + dx},${y + dy}`;\n      counts.set(key, (counts.get(key) ?? 0) + 1);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  const next: Cell[] = [];\n  for (const [key, count] of counts) {\n    const wasAlive = live.has(key);\n    if (count === 3 || (count === 2 && wasAlive)) {\n      next.push(fromKey(key));\n    }\n  }\n  return next;\n}\n```\n\nThis is clean: keeps everything in one function, but unifies the rules. Let me go with this.\n\nActually, wait. Let me think about one more thing: should I extract a `countNeighbors` helper? The current main function is 16 lines. Extracting makes it ~10 lines. The extraction adds a 7-line function. Net change: similar size but better organization.\n\nYou know what, let me just commit to a refactor and move on. Here's my plan:\n\n1. Unify the survival and birth rules into one expression\n2. Single iteration over candidates\n3. Keep the helper functions (toKey, fromKey) as they are\n4. Add a clarifying comment about the unified rule\n\nLet me apply this.\n\nActually, I realize one more thing. The original code's `NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS` type is `ReadonlyArray<readonly [number, number]>` but we're using `[number, number]` destructure in the for loop. This works fine but we could simplify.\n\nOK let me just do the refactor.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"I'll analyze the code and perform a refactoring pass.\n\n## Step 1: Naming Evaluation\n\n- **Current name**: `nextGeneration` - Takes current generation of cells, returns next. Name fits perfectly.\n- **Helper names**: `toKey` / `fromKey` - clear, idiomatic\n- **No rename needed**.\n\n## Step 2: Calculate Initial APP Mass\n\nLooking at the current implementation:\n\n```\nfunction nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  const live = new Set(cells.map(toKey));\n  const counts = new Map<string, number>();\n\n  for (const [x, y] of cells) {                                    // loop 1\n    for (const [dx, dy] of NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS) {                     // loop 2\n      const key = `${x + dx},${y + dy}`;                           // template invocation\n      counts.set(key, (counts.get(key) ?? 0) + 1);                 // invocation + ?? 0 + + 1\n    }\n  }\n\n  const next: Cell[] = [];\n  for (const cell of cells) {                                      // loop 3\n    const count = counts.get(toKey(cell)) ?? 0;\n    if (count === 2 || count === 3) next.push(cell);               // conditional\n  }\n  for (const [key, count] of counts) {                             // loop 4\n    if (count === 3 && !live.has(key)) next.push(fromKey(key));    // conditional\n  }\n  return next;\n}\n```\n\n**Current Mass** (rough count):\n- Constants (0, 1, 2, 2, 3, 3): 6\n- Bindings (cells, live, counts, next, x, y, dx, dy, key, cell, count): 11\n- Invocations (new Set, cells.map, toKey, new Map, template, counts.set, counts.get, counts.get, toKey, counts.get, live.has, next.push, next.push, fromKey): 14 × 2 = 28\n- Conditionals (`count===2||count===3`, `count===3`): 2 × 4 = 8\n- Loops (4 nested iterations): 4 × 5 = 20\n- Assignments (counts.set, next.push ×2): 3 × 6 = 18\n\n**Total ≈ 91**\n\n## Step 3: Apply Simple Design Rules\n\n### Rule 1: Tests Pass ✓ (verified above, 7 passed)\n\n### Rule 2: Reveals Intent — opportunity found\nThe two iterations (survive loop, born loop) duplicate the conceptual question \"what's the next state of this cell?\". Game of Life has a beautiful unified rule: **a cell is alive next iff it has 3 live neighbors, OR it was already alive with exactly 2**. The current code obscures this with two separate paths.\n\n### Rule 3: No Duplication — opportunity found\nBoth the \"survive\" and \"born\" loops iterate candidates and apply a conditional on the count. They can be unified.\n\n### Rule 4: Fewest Elements — opportunity found\nTwo loops can become one; one less conditional.\n\n## Step 4: Apply Refactoring\n\nI'll unify the rules and reduce two loops to one:"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_3fedf744a7144891816712b5","name":"edit","arguments":{"edits":[{"newText":"/**\n * Computes the next generation of live cells according to Conway's\n * Game of Life rules:\n *   1. Any live cell with fewer than 2 live neighbours dies (underpopulation).\n *   2. Any live cell with 2 or 3 live neighbours lives on (survival).\n *   3. Any live cell with more than 3 live neighbours dies (overpopulation).\n *   4. Any dead cell with exactly 3 live neighbours becomes alive (reproduction).\n *\n * Equivalently, a cell is alive in the next generation iff it has exactly 3\n * live neighbours, or it was already alive with exactly 2.\n *\n * @param cells The set of live cells in the current generation.\n * @returns The set of live cells in the next generation.\n */\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  const live = new Set(cells.map(toKey));\n  const counts = new Map<string, number>();\n\n  for (const [x, y] of cells) {\n    for (const [dx, dy] of NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS) {\n      const key = `${x + dx},${y + dy}`;\n      counts.set(key, (counts.get(key) ?? 0) + 1);\n    }\n  }\n\n  const next: Cell[] = [];\n  for (const [key, count] of counts) {\n    const wasAlive = live.has(key);\n    if (count === 3 || (count === 2 && wasAlive)) {\n      next.push(fromKey(key));\n    }\n  }\n  return next;\n}","oldText":"/**\n * Computes the next generation of live cells according to Conway's\n * Game of Life rules:\n *   1. Any live cell with fewer than 2 live neighbours dies (underpopulation).\n *   2. Any live cell with 2 or 3 live neighbours lives on (survival).\n *   3. Any live cell with more than 3 live neighbours dies (overpopulation).\n *   4. Any dead cell with exactly 3 live neighbours becomes alive (reproduction).\n *\n * @param cells The set of live cells in the current generation.\n * @returns The set of live cells in the next generation.\n */\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  const live = new Set(cells.map(toKey));\n  const counts = new Map<string, number>();\n\n  for (const [x, y] of cells) {\n    for (const [dx, dy] of NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS) {\n      const key = `${x + dx},${y + dy}`;\n      counts.set(key, (counts.get(key) ?? 0) + 1);\n    }\n  }\n\n  const next: Cell[] = [];\n  for (const cell of cells) {\n    const count = counts.get(toKey(cell)) ?? 0;\n    if (count === 2 || count === 3) next.push(cell);\n  }\n  for (const [key, count] of counts) {\n    if (count === 3 && !live.has(key)) next.push(fromKey(key));\n  }\n  return next;\n}"}],"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_09-27-34_game-of-life-example-mapping_v6.2.1-phase-continuation-pi_minimax-m3/src/game-of-life.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":279,"output":10566,"cacheRead":8192,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":9103,"totalTokens":19037,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784973371237,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-4fb47f89-a0ef-4e05-baac-1942e1d505f1","responseModel":"minimax/minimax-m3"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_3fedf744a7144891816712b5","toolName":"edit","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully replaced 1 block(s) in /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_09-27-34_game-of-life-example-mapping_v6.2.1-phase-continuation-pi_minimax-m3/src/game-of-life.t