cells.map(toKey));\n  const counts = new Map<string, number>();\n\n  for (const [x, y] of cells) {\n    for (const [dx, dy] of NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS) {\n      const key = toKey([x + dx, y + dy]);\n      counts.set(key, (counts.get(key) ?? 0) + 1);\n    }\n  }\n\n  const next: Cell[] = [];\n  for (const [key, count] of counts) {\n    if (survives(count, live.has(key))) {\n      next.push(fromKey(key));\n    }\n  }\n  return next;\n}\n```\n\nNow let me calculate the new APP mass.\n\nActually, let me run the tests first with the current implementation to confirm baseline.\n\nLet me do this:\n1. First, confirm tests pass with current code (done — 11 passing)\n2. Apply refactoring\n3. Run tests again to confirm they still pass\n4. Calculate mass before/after\n\nLet me also make sure the refactoring is necessary or beneficial. Looking at the current code one more time...\n\nActually, you know what, I want to think about this more carefully. The current code is already pretty clean. Let me list what's there:\n\n- `Cell` type — appropriate\n- `NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS` — appropriate, well-documented\n- `toKey`/`fromKey` — necessary helpers\n- `nextGeneration` — the main function\n\nThe function body has:\n1. Build a set of live cell keys (1 line)\n2. Initialize a count map (1 line)\n3. Nested loop to count neighbors (5 lines)\n4. Initialize result array (1 line)\n5. Loop through counts and apply survival rule (5 lines)\n6. Return result (1 line)\n\nIt's about 13 lines. Reasonable for the algorithm.\n\nWhat could be improved:\n1. **The conditional `count === 3 || (count === 2 && wasAlive)`** — this is the densest part. A `survives` helper would help.\n2. **The duplicate key format** between `toKey` and the template literal in the inner loop — minor DRY issue.\n3. **The `wasAlive` binding** — it adds clarity by naming the result of `live.has(key)`. If we inline `live.has(key)` into `survives(count, live.has(key))`, we lose the name but gain conciseness.\n\nLet me think about (3). `wasAlive` is a self-documenting name. Inlining it loses that. But the call to `survives(count, live.has(key))` is still readable because `live.has(key)` clearly returns a boolean indicating if the key was in the live set.\n\nI think the trade-off is acceptable. Let me go with the refactoring.\n\nLet me also verify my understanding of `survives`:\n- `survives(0, false)` = `0 === 3 || (0 === 2 && false)` = `false || (false && false)` = `false` ✓ (cell dies)\n- `survives(0, true)` = `0 === 3 || (0 === 2 && true)` = `false || (false && true)` = `false` ✓ (cell dies, underpopulation)\n- `survives(1, false)` = `1 === 3 || (1 === 2 && false)` = `false || false` = `false` ✓\n- `survives(1, true)` = `1 === 3 || (1 === 2 && true)` = `false || (false && true)` = `false` ✓\n- `survives(2, false)` = `2 === 3 || (2 === 2 && false)` = `false || false` = `false` ✓\n- `survives(2, true)` = `2 === 3 || (2 === 2 && true)` = `false || true` = `true` ✓ (survival)\n- `survives(3, false)` = `3 === 3 || ...` = `true` ✓ (birth)\n- `survives(3, true)` = `3 === 3 || ...` = `true` ✓ (survival)\n- `survives(4, true)` = `4 === 3 || (4 === 2 && true)` = `false || false` = `false` ✓ (overpopulation)\n\nGreat, `survives` correctly captures the rules.\n\nNow let me make the edit.\n\nActually, wait. Let me think one more time. Is there anything I'm missing? Let me look at the test file again for any specific patterns or naming that I should follow.\n\nLooking at the tests:\n- They use `it(\"should ...\", () => { ... })` pattern\n- They use `.sort()` to compare arrays regardless of order\n- They use `Array<[number, number]>` in some places\n\nOK, nothing special to follow.\n\nOne more consideration: the `survives` function name. Is `survives` the best name? It implies the cell already existed and continues to exist. But `survives` also works for \"becomes alive\" in a loose sense (a cell that comes into being is \"born\", not \"survives\").\n\nAlternative names:\n- `shouldBeAlive(count, wasAlive)` — more explicit, captures both survival and birth\n- `nextAlive(count, wasAlive)` — clear\n- `lives(count, wasAlive)` — short, but might be confused with the function name\n\nI think `shouldBeAlive` is more accurate, but `survives` is shorter and matches the Game of Life terminology. Let me use `shouldBeAlive` for clarity — it's more descriptive of what the function returns.\n\nWait, the JSDoc says \"Returns true iff a cell should be alive next generation\". So `shouldBeAlive` matches the JSDoc. But actually, I think `survives` is fine too — it captures the intuition.\n\nHmm, let me think. The Game of Life has these rules:\n1. Live cell with 2-3 neighbors survives\n2. Dead cell with 3 neighbors is born\n3. Other cells die or stay dead\n\nThe function `survives(count, wasAlive)` returns true in cases 1 and 2. So it's not strictly \"survives\" — it's \"lives in the next generation\". `shouldBeAlive` or `livesNext` might be better.\n\nLet me go with `shouldBeAlive` for precision. Or maybe `isAliveNext`?\n\nActually, `shouldBeAlive` is the most natural English. Let me use that.\n\nFinal refactoring:\n\n```typescript\nconst shouldBeAlive = (count: number, wasAlive: boolean): boolean =>\n  count === 3 || (count === 2 && wasAlive);\n```\n\nHmm, but this name is also not super crisp. Let me consider one more time.\n\nHow about `livesOn` or `isAliveNext`?\n- `livesOn` is idiomatic in Game of Life context\n- `isAliveNext` is descriptive\n\nOr we could name it based on the algorithm: `nextState(count, wasAlive)`.\n\nYou know what, let me just go with `shouldBeAlive`. It's clear and explicit.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider once more. The convention in the existing code uses verbs like `toKey`, `fromKey`, `nextGeneration`. A verb-like name like `isAlive` or `survives` would fit better than `shouldBeAlive`.\n\nLet me go with `survives`. Even though technically it also covers birth (a dead cell with 3 neighbors becomes alive), in Game of Life parlance \"survive\" is often used loosely. Or I could use `isAliveNext(count, wasAlive)`.\n\nOK final decision: `survives`. It's the most concise and Game-of-Life-natural name.\n\nActually no, let me go with `shouldBeAlive` because it's the most accurate description of what the function returns. The JSDoc explains the Game of Life rules and links the function to them.\n\nOK I'm going to stop second-guessing and just make the decision. Let me use `shouldBeAlive`.\n\nHmm wait. Let me think about this from the perspective of code reading. When you see:\n```typescript\nif (shouldBeAlive(count, live.has(key))) {\n  next.push(fromKey(key));\n}\n```\n\nvs\n\n```typescript\nif (survives(count, live.has(key))) {\n  next.push(fromKey(key));\n}\n```\n\nBoth are clear. `shouldBeAlive` is more literal, `survives` is more Game-of-Life-flavored.\n\nI'll go with `survives` because:\n1. It's shorter\n2. \"Survives\" in Game of Life context includes both survival and birth (a cell that's \"still there\" next generation)\n3. It matches the JSDoc's framing\n\nActually no, let me re-read the JSDoc I'm planning to write:\n\n```\n/**\n * Returns true iff a cell should be alive next generation given its\n * live-neighbor count and whether it was alive this generation.\n *\n * Captures the Game of Life rules compactly:\n *   - A live cell with 2 or 3 neighbours survives.\n *   - A dead cell with exactly 3 neighbours is born.\n *   - All other cells die or stay dead.\n */\n```\n\nHmm, this JSDoc describes what the function does, which is \"decides if a cell should be alive next generation\". The name should reflect this. `shouldBeAlive` is the most direct mapping.\n\nOK let me commit: `shouldBeAlive`. Final answer.\n\nActually, I realize I'm spending too much time on the naming. Let me just pick something and go. The refactoring's primary purpose is to extract the complex boolean logic. The name is secondary.\n\nI'll go with `survives` because it's shorter and the JSDoc explains the broader meaning.\n\nOK, let me make the edits now. I'll commit to:\n- Function name: `survives`\n- Inner loop: use `toKey` consistently\n\nLet me also think about whether I should remove the JSDoc on `nextGeneration`. Currently it has the 4 rules AND the equivalent formulation. With `survives` documented, the equivalent formulation is captured in the helper. But the 4 rules are still useful as a top-level overview.\n\nI'll keep the JSDoc as-is.\n\nLet me write the edits now.\n\nWait, one more thing. The current code uses `${x + dx},${y + dy}` template literal. If I change it to `toKey([x + dx, y + dy])`, I'm allocating a temporary array `[x + dx, y + dy]` just to pass it to `toKey`. This is slightly less efficient than the template literal.\n\nHmm. Let me think about whether to optimize this. The hot path is the inner loop, which runs 8 times per cell. For a typical Game of Life grid, this could be many calls.\n\nBut the toKey function just does `${x},${y}`, which is the same template literal. So:\n- Current: `${x + dx},${y + dy}` — 1 string allocation\n- New: `toKey([x + dx, y + dy])` — 1 array allocation + 1 string allocation + 1 function call\n\nThe new version has 1 extra allocation per iteration. For most use cases this is negligible.\n\nBut for \"fewest elements\" (Rule 4), keeping the template literal might be slightly preferred. However, \"no duplication\" (Rule 3) is higher priority.\n\nActually, the duplication is the format string `${x},${y}` vs `${x + dx},${y + dy}`. They're not quite the same — one uses interpolated variables directly, the other uses `x + dx` and `y + dy`. If the format ever changes (e.g., we wanted to use a different separator), we'd need to update both places.\n\nLet me go with `toKey` for DRY. The performance impact is negligible.\n\nOK, let me make the edits.\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider once more. Let me look at the test that exercises many cells:\n- Test 11: two blocks (4+4 = 8 cells), 8 neighbors each = 64 neighbor counts\n\nThat's not many. Performance is fine either way.\n\nLet me proceed with the refactoring.\n\nOne more thought: I could also consider whether to use a single loop structure or stay with the current nested loop. For example:\n```typescript\nfor (const [x, y] of cells) {\n  for (const [dx, dy] of NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS) {\n    const key = toKey([x + dx, y + dy]);\n    counts.set(key, (counts.get(key) ?? 0) + 1);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nvs\n```typescript\ncells.forEach(([x, y]) => {\n  NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS.forEach(([dx, dy]) => {\n    const key = toKey([x + dx, y + dy]);\n    counts.set(key, (counts.get(key) ?? 0) + 1);\n  });\n});\n```\n\nThe first is more concise and uses for...of which is well-supported. Let me keep for...of.\n\nOK, final plan:\n\n1. Extract `survives(count, wasAlive)` helper\n2. Use `toKey` consistently in the inner loop\n3. Move JSDoc on `nextGeneration` (keep it as is)\n\nLet me make the edit now.\n\nActually, on reflection, let me think about whether to extract `survives` one more time.\n\nPros of extracting:\n- Improves intent at the call site\n- Reusable (in case we want to test it independently)\n- The complex `||`/`&&` logic is isolated and can be documented\n\nCons of extracting:\n- Adds an abstraction (Rule 4: Fewest Elements)\n- Used only once\n\nThe rule says \"Only add complexity when it serves clear purpose.\" Does this serve a clear purpose? Yes — it makes the conditional at the call site read like natural language: `if (survives(count, live.has(key)))`. Without it, the reader has to mentally translate `count === 3 || (count === 2 && wasAlive)` to \"survives\".\n\nI'll proceed with the extraction.\n\nLet me write the edit.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Looking at the implementation file now."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_6fdd2dcfacc34edbaf0f0671","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"pnpm test 2>&1 | tail -20"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":306,"output":12288,"cacheRead":8832,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":12245