unts.set(key, (counts.get(key) ?? 0) + 1);\n    }\n  }\n\n  const next: Cell[] = [];\n  for (const [key, count] of counts) {\n    const wasAlive = live.has(key);\n    if (count === 3 || (count === 2 && wasAlive)) {\n      next.push(fromKey(key));\n    }\n  }\n  return next;\n}\n```\n\n**Component Count (Original)**:\n- Constants: 16 (NEIGHBOR values) + `3`, `2`, `0`, `1` (4) + format literals in toKey/fromKey (~2) = **22** × 1 = 22\n- Bindings: `cells`, `live`, `counts`, `x`, `y`, `dx`, `dy`, `key`, `next`, `key`, `count`, `wasAlive` + `toKey`/`fromKey` params (4) = **16** × 1 = 16\n- Invocations: `cells.map`, `toKey` (callback), `counts.set`, `counts.get`, `live.has`, `next.push`, `fromKey` = **7** × 2 = 14\n- Conditionals: `??` nullish coalescing + `if` with `||`/`&&` = **2** × 4 = 8\n- Loops: outer + inner + result loop = **3** × 5 = 15\n- Assignments: `live =`, `counts =`, `key =`, `next =`, `wasAlive =`, plus mutations via `counts.set` and `next.push` = **7** × 6 = 42\n\n**Total Mass (Original): 22 + 16 + 14 + 8 + 15 + 42 = 117**\n\n## Step 3: Apply Simple Design Rules\n\n### Rule 1: Tests Pass ✓\nAll 11 tests pass.\n\n### Rule 2: Reveals Intent\nThe conditional `count === 3 || (count === 2 && wasAlive)` is dense. A reader needs Game of Life knowledge to decode it. Extracting `survives(count, wasAlive)` would make the intent self-documenting.\n\n### Rule 3: No Duplication (DRY)\nThe key format `${x},${y}` exists in `toKey` and is duplicated in the inner loop as `${x + dx},${y + dy}`. These two places must stay in sync if the format ever changes. Using `toKey` consistently eliminates this duplication.\n\n### Rule 4: Fewest Elements\nCurrent element count is reasonable. Adding one helper (`survives`) is justified by the intent gain.\n\n## Step 4: Implement Refactoring\n\nTwo improvements:\n1. Extract `survives(count, wasAlive)` helper\n2. Use `toKey` consistently in the inner loop\n\nLet me apply them.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","tex