e call site reads naturally: `if (survives(count, live.has(key)))`.\n3. The helper is self-documenting with its JSDoc.\n\nArguments against:\n1. It's only used once.\n2. The original is small and inline.\n\nI think the pro outweighs the con here. The helper name `survives` directly conveys the Game of Life concept, and the call site is cleaner.\n\nOK let me also think about whether there are any other improvements I could make:\n\n1. The `survives` function could be tested in isolation (but we don't have such a test, and adding one would be feature creep).\n2. We could extract `countNeighbors` into its own function (but it's only used once and is fairly small).\n3. We could simplify the `counts.set(key, (counts.get(key) ?? 0) + 1)` pattern (but this is idiomatic and clear).\n\nI'll stop here. The refactoring is complete.\n\nActually, let me reconsider once more whether to extract `countNeighbors`:\n\nCurrent:\n```typescript\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  const live = new Set(cells.map(toKey));\n  const counts = new Map<string, number>();\n\n  for (const [x, y] of cells) {\n    for (const [dx, dy] of NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS) {\n      const key = toKey([x + dx, y + dy]);\n      counts.set(key, (counts.get(key) ?? 0) + 1);\n    }\n  }\n\n  const next: Cell[] = [];\n  for (const [key, count] of counts) {\n    if (survives(count, live.has(key))) {\n      next.push(fromKey(key));\n    }\n  }\n  return next;\n}\n```\n\nIf I extract countNeighbors:\n```typescript\nconst countNeighbors = (cells: Cell[], counts: Map<string, number>): void => {\n  for (const [x, y] of cells) {\n    for (const [dx, dy] of NEIGHBOR_OFFSETS) {\n      const key = toKey([x + dx, y + dy]);\n      counts.set(key, (counts.get(key) ?? 0) + 1);\n    }\n  }\n};\n\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  const live = new Set(cells.map(toKey));\n  const counts = new Map<string, number>();\n  countNeighbors(cells, counts);\n\n  const next: Cell[] = [];\n  for (const [key, count] of counts) {\n    if (survives(count, live.has(key))) {\n      next.push(fromKey(key));\n    }\n  }\n  return next;\n}\n```\n\nThe `nextGeneration` becomes a clean pipeline:\n1. Build live set\n2. Count neighbors\n3. Filter by survival rule\n\nThis is more intent-revealing. But adds another helper.\n\nHmm. I'll think about this. The Rule 4 says \"Only add complexity when it serves clear purpose.\" Does extracting `countNeighbors` serve a clear purpose? Yes — it makes the high-level algorithm steps visible.\n\nBut it also adds another function, which might be over-engineering. Let me not extract `countNeighbors` for now. The current refactoring is sufficient.\n\nActually, looking at this more, I think the function is fine as is. The nested loop is only 4 lines and is clearly doing neighbor counting. Extracting it adds another level of indirection without much clarity gain.\n\nOK I'm satisfied with the refactoring. Let me verify the tests one more time and write up the report.\n\nActually, looking at the JSDoc on `nextGeneration`, I notice it already includes the equivalent formulation: \"Equivalently, a cell is alive in the next generation iff it has exactly 3 live neighbours, or it was already alive with exactly 2.\"\n\nThis is exactly what `survives` encodes! So the JSDoc and the helper are nicely connected.\n\nAlright, I'm done with the refactoring. Let me also double-check the tests pass.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"All 11 tests pass with the refactored code.\n\n## Step 5: New APP Mass\n\n**Component Count (Refactored)**:\n- **Constants** (×1): 16 NEIGHBOR values + `3`, `2` in `survives` + `0`, `1` in increment + format separators = **22**\n- **Bindings** (×1): `cells`, `live`, `counts`, `x`, `y`, `dx`, `dy`, `key`, `next`, `key`, `count`, `survives`, + helper params = **17**\n- **Invocations** (×2): `cells.map`, `toKey`(×2), `counts.set`, `counts.get`, `live.has`, `survives`, `next.push`, `fromKey` = **9 × 2 = 18